bodyText:
Section 76-1421 imposes eight duties on every Nebraska residential tenant whether or not your lease repeats them. The tenant shall:
- (1) comply with all obligations primarily imposed upon tenants by applicable minimum standards of building and housing codes materially affecting health or safety;
- (2) keep the part of the premises the tenant occupies and uses as clean and safe as the condition of the premises permits, 'and upon termination of the tenancy place the dwelling unit in as clean condition, excepting ordinary wear and tear, as when the tenancy commenced';
- (3) dispose from the dwelling unit all ashes, rubbish, garbage and other waste in a clean and safe manner;
- (4) keep all plumbing fixtures in the unit or used by the tenant as clean as their condition permits;
- (5) use in a reasonable manner all electrical, plumbing, sanitary, heating, ventilating, air conditioning and other facilities and appliances including elevators;
- (6) 'not deliberately or negligently destroy, deface, damage, impair or remove any part of the premises or knowingly permit any person to do so';
- (7) conduct himself and 'require other persons on the premises with his consent' to conduct themselves in a manner that will not disturb neighbours' peaceful enjoyment;
- (8) abide by all bylaws, covenants, rules or regulations of any applicable condominium regime, cooperative housing agreement or neighbourhood association not inconsistent with the landlord's rights or duties.

THREE CONSEQUENCES WORTH DRAFTING AROUND. First, you do not need to restate these to enforce them. Section 76-1431(1) gives you the termination route for 'a noncompliance with section 76-1421 materially affecting health and safety or a material noncompliance by the tenant with the rental agreement or any separate agreement' - the statute is already a named ground - and section 76-1431(3) gives you damages and injunctive relief 'for any noncompliance by the tenant with the rental agreement or section 76-1421', with reasonable attorney's fees if the tenant's noncompliance is willful. Copying section 76-1421 into the lease adds nothing to your remedies; what it adds is clarity for the tenant, and that is the only reason to do it.

Second, subdivision (2) is your deposit-deduction hook. The duty to return the unit as clean as when the tenancy commenced, 'excepting ordinary wear and tear', is what section 76-1416(2) measures a damages deduction against - which is why a dated move-in condition record is worth more than any lease wording.

Third, subdivision (7) is the guest-conduct duty, and note the Act's phrase: 'other persons on the premises with his consent'. The word 'guest' appears nowhere in sections 76-1401 to 76-1449 except in section 76-1431, so draft and argue in the statute's words, not in the word guest.

WHAT THE LEASE STILL HAS TO DO. Anything beyond the eight - no smoking, parking, quiet hours, trash days, pet terms, lawn care - comes from your own terms or from house rules, and house rules are enforceable 'as provided in section 76-1431 against the tenant only if' they meet all six conditions in section 76-1422: a qualifying purpose, reasonable relation to it, application 'to all tenants in the premises in a fair manner', sufficient explicitness to fairly inform, not for the purpose of evading your own obligations, and - the one that defeats most of them - 'the tenant has notice of it at the time he enters into the rental agreement'. A rule adopted later is enforceable only 'if reasonable notice of its adoption is given to the tenant and it does not work a substantial modification of his bargain'.

And moving any part of YOUR section 76-1419 duty onto the tenant is a separate exercise with its own narrow conditions in sections 76-1419(2) and (3), not something a tenant-duties clause can do.
